---
import SiteLayout from "../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import FlavorTabs from "../../../components/FlavorTabs.astro";
import ModuleHero from "../../../components/module/ModuleHero.astro";
import ModuleScenario from "../../../components/module/ModuleScenario.astro";
import ModuleFlowDiagram from "../../../components/module/ModuleFlowDiagram.astro";
import ServingHubDiagram from "../../../components/module/ServingHubDiagram.astro";
import CitationTraceDemo from "../../../components/module/demos/CitationTraceDemo.astro";
import ModuleFooterNav from "../../../components/module/ModuleFooterNav.astro";
import ModuleRecap from "../../../components/module/ModuleRecap.astro";
import { flavor as gcp } from "../../../data/flavors/gcp";
import { flavor as aws } from "../../../data/flavors/aws";
import { flavor as azure } from "../../../data/flavors/azure";
import { flavor as oss } from "../../../data/flavors/oss";
import { MODULES } from "../../../data/modules";

const moduleId = "25-serving";
const entries = {
  gcp: gcp[moduleId],
  aws: aws[moduleId],
  azure: azure[moduleId],
  oss: oss[moduleId],
};
const moduleData = MODULES.find((m) => m.id === moduleId)!;
---

<SiteLayout title="25 · Serving a RAG API" description={moduleData.summary}>
  <article>
    <ModuleHero moduleId={moduleId}>
      <p>
        Ingestion, chunking, embeddings — everything so far happens offline, once per document. This module wires
        the online path: what happens the instant a user asks a question.
      </p>
    </ModuleHero>

    <ModuleScenario moduleId={moduleId} />

    <ModuleFlowDiagram moduleId={moduleId} />

    <ServingHubDiagram />

    <h2>The request path</h2>
    <div class="concept-flow" role="list" aria-label="RAG request path">
      <span class="chip chip--accent" role="listitem">EMBED</span>
      <span class="concept-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <span class="chip" role="listitem">RETRIEVE</span>
      <span class="concept-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <span class="chip" role="listitem">CONSTRUCT PROMPT</span>
      <span class="concept-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <span class="chip" role="listitem">CALL LLM</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>Embed</strong> the incoming question with the same model used in Module 20. <strong>Retrieve</strong> the nearest chunks from the vector index. <strong>Construct a prompt</strong> with the question plus the
      retrieved chunks, structured so the model can cite which chunk supports which claim. <strong>Call the LLM</strong> — a fast model by default, escalating to a reasoning model only when the task needs it (Module 75 covers that
      trade-off in cost terms).
    </p>

    <h2>Citations aren't optional</h2>
    <p>
      A RAG answer without a citation to its source chunk is a claim you can't verify. Return the chunk ID (or
      document + offset) alongside every answer — free, since retrieval already has that metadata — and it's the
      difference between "an AI said so" and "here's the paragraph it came from."
    </p>

    <CitationTraceDemo />

    <h2>Handling "I don't know"</h2>
    <p>
      When retrieval returns nothing relevant, the model should say so, not answer from general knowledge as if it
      had a source. Guard: if the top chunk's similarity score is below a threshold, skip the LLM call and return a
      plain "not found in the indexed documents" response.
    </p>

    <h2>Streaming: return tokens as they're generated</h2>
    <p>
      A blocking call makes users stare at a spinner for the model's full generation time. <strong>Streaming</strong> returns tokens as the model produces them, so the first word appears in a few hundred milliseconds instead of
      after the whole answer — the same time-to-first-token idea Module 55 measures, applied here as the response
      mechanism, not just a metric. A citation-bearing answer still streams fine: send the chunk IDs just before the
      streamed text or as a trailing event once generation finishes.
    </p>

    <h2>One call here, many calls in an agent loop</h2>
    <p>
      This module's whole job: one embed, one retrieve, one generate, one response. Module 85's agent loop reuses
      this exact request path as a single tool call inside its plan-act-observe loop — a user question there can
      trigger it several times before the loop produces a final answer. Multiply this module's latency by however
      many iterations the loop runs to get the agent's actual end-to-end latency.
    </p>

    <h2>Try it in your flavor</h2>
    <FlavorTabs moduleId={moduleId} entries={entries} />

    <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No citation in the response — makes every answer unverifiable and erodes trust the moment it's wrong once.</li>
      <li>Letting the model answer when retrieval found nothing relevant — this is where hallucination gets blamed on "the AI" when it's actually a missing guard.</li>
      <li>Skipping request logging at launch "to add later" — Module 30's event log needs data from day one, and you can't retroactively log a request that already happened.</li>
      <li>Defaulting to the most expensive/slowest model for every query — start fast-tier, escalate only when needed.</li>
      <li>Building a blocking, non-streamed API because it's simpler, then wondering why the UI feels slow even though total latency looks fine on a dashboard.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
  <ModuleRecap moduleId={moduleId} />
  <ModuleFooterNav moduleId={moduleId} />
</SiteLayout>
